          "At the same time, love sometimes limits its freedom for others’ sake. Paul would give up meat rather than cause a weaker believer to fall (1 Cor 8:13), and he became “all things to all men” so that he might win some (1 Cor 9:19-23). “Let no one seek his own, but each one his neighbor’s good” (1 Cor 10:24). Notice the reason: someone’s real good or faith, not merely their wish to have everything their way.",
          "Daniel offers a helpful model. In Babylon he did not want the king’s food. He neither exploded nor silently gave in; he asked respectfully for an alternative and proposed a test (Dan 1:8-16). Ruth, in contrast, freely chose to adopt Naomi’s people and God (Ruth 1:16). Both are honoured: honest preference expressed with grace, and preference given up in love."
